+\section{Matched CPU comparisons}\label{app:cpu-controls}
+These supporting comparisons preserve each workload, paired reference, resource
+configuration, native outcome exception and sampling limitation. Their separate
+blocks do not establish a global CPU ranking or a heterogeneous speedup.
+
+\paragraph{Complete CPU compilation pairs.}
+A separate matched four-CPU block checks every one of the 29,750 released
+GoedelWorkbook sources with the original GC service and the same service
+loading native-compiled Mathlib modules~\cite{lean4}.
+Whole elapsed time is 32,945.98 versus 15,835.68 seconds in the first pair
+and 38,443.59 versus 18,306.13 seconds in the reversed-order second pair.
+These are reductions of 51.93\% and 52.38\%.
+Runtime import and library loading are charged, while software build is excluded.
+In the first pair, native fields match on 29,725 sources after excluding the
+adapter timer. Sixteen operational outcomes become complete and nine complete
+outcomes become operational. The second pair has 21 operational-to-complete
+transitions and 12 reverse transitions. One additional source is operational
+in both but changes from a decoder failure to a timeout; the other 29,716
+native payloads match except the adapter timer.
+No complete/rejected flip is observed, and failures remain charged.
+The third alternating pair and separate profiles remain outstanding.
+These paired observations strengthen an established CPU baseline. They do not
+establish a final replicated effect, a new compiler contribution or GPU acceleration.
+Clocks from other CPU or oracle blocks are not used as denominators.
+
+A separate producer-compatible Lean4.9 GC comparison processes all 7,808
+retained miniF2F source attempts per method with and without selective native
+Linarith compilation. All three primary pairs finish: 12,689.28 versus
+11,723.45 seconds, 12,539.74 versus 11,192.98 seconds, and 11,914.91 versus
+9,893.72 seconds. Whole elapsed reductions are 7.61\%, 10.74\% and 16.96\%;
+their descriptive mean is 11.77\%, without a confidence interval.
+Request p99 rises from 247.85 to 275.35 seconds and from 251.14 to 253.28 seconds
+in the first two pairs, but falls from 224.27 to 209.72 seconds in the third.
+The complete count remains 3,008 in all six conditions.
+The second pair has two operational-to-rejected transitions and five other
+native diagnostic differences; the third has three such transitions and one
+operational diagnostic difference. Remaining payloads match except the adapter
+timer. Lower whole elapsed therefore does not establish uniform tail latency,
+transparent native responses or GPU benefit. The separate resource comparison
+remains outstanding. Its producer version and selective compiled closure differ from the
+Goedel block above.
+
+We also compare complete verification with and without reuse of resolved
+multiplication-theorem applications within each nonlinear arithmetic preprocessing invocation.
+Against the same FarkasFast/native-hybrid route, three complete 29,750-source
+pairs reduce whole elapsed time from 11,629.90 to 10,487.44 seconds,
+12,852.84 to 11,208.51 seconds and 14,753.25 to 12,483.33 seconds.
+The observed reductions are 9.82\%, 12.79\% and 15.39\%, with a descriptive mean
+of 12.67\%. This observed range is not a confidence interval.
+Closed-loop request p99 also falls in each pair, from 10.56 to 10.09, 12.16 to
+10.73 and 14.45 to 12.00 seconds. These are response distributions within the
+measured blocks, not an offered-load capacity result.
+The third pair retains six operational/completion transitions and otherwise
+matching native payloads. No complete/rejected transition occurs.
+All three primary pairs retaining the original arithmetic oracle instead take
+15,385.79 versus 15,475.96 seconds, 16,245.63 versus 15,415.52 seconds, and
+14,942.76 versus 12,805.48 seconds with reuse. Whole time is 0.59\% longer, then
+5.11\% and 14.30\% lower; the descriptive mean reduction is 6.28\%, without a
+confidence interval. The third pair has five operational-to-complete and five
+reverse transitions; other native payloads match except the adapter timer.
+No complete/rejected transition occurs. Their timing signs differ.
+The different blocks and shared background do not isolate a causal oracle
+interaction or establish a uniform original-route gain.
+The Fast/hybrid comparison now completes all eight conditions, including
+two separate resource captures. Observed sampled peak PSS falls from
+7,674,160 to 6,961,995 KiB, with incomplete reads in 33 of 7,051 and 34 of
+6,198 non-atomic samples. These single profiles do not establish exact peaks
+or replicated memory savings. Seven operational/completion transitions remain.
+Two separate original-oracle resource profiles remain outstanding.
+These observations concern an ordinary CPU optimization within its matched
+block. They do not establish the globally strongest CPU method, systems novelty
+or GPU acceleration.
+
+\paragraph{Complete native arithmetic primary series.}
+Three matched Goedel CPU pairs compare compiled Mathlib with the same service
+using the native exact arithmetic oracle. Reference whole times are
+10,531.82, 13,112.67 and 15,097.58 seconds. Candidate times are
+11,030.02, 10,666.44 and 14,886.85 seconds.
+The candidate is 4.73\% slower, then 18.66\% and 1.40\% lower in elapsed time.
+The descriptive mean reduction of 5.11\% is not a confidence interval or a
+stable general advantage. Pair signs differ and the same-code references drift.
+Each condition returns all 29,750 inputs. The third pair retains 13 native
+outcome differences, all involving an operational outcome, with no direct
+complete/rejected transition. Operational failures stay charged.
+All eight conditions, including the two separate resource profiles, now finish.
+In the single profile pair, observed sampled peak PSS rises from 6,561,664 to
+7,478,115 KiB. Incomplete, non-atomic sampling does not establish exact or
+replicated peaks. This pair shows no measured memory saving.
+These complete comparisons preserve a competent CPU competitor. They do not
+establish the globally strongest CPU path or heterogeneous acceleration.
+
+For these two complete CPU comparisons, we divide each candidate's whole
+time by its own paired reference time. A ratio below one means lower elapsed
+time. Figure~\ref{fig:cpu-paired-whole-time} displays all three primary
+observations per comparison: product reuse has the same direction in every
+pair, while the native exact oracle has mixed directions. The rows use
+different references and CPU eligibility, so they do not rank methods across
+comparisons.
+
+\begin{figure}[t]
+\centering
+\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/cpu-paired-whole-time.pdf}
+\caption{Whole-time ratios for two complete CPU comparisons, each processing
